  "passageActivities": {
    "correction": [
      {
        "textOverride": "The genomics revolution make it possible to see just how impactful touch is to plants on a deeper level. Peering at the genes of Arabidopsis thaliana, a weedy plant in the mustard family and the lab rat of the plant biology world, researchers see that touch quietly triggered such a dramatic response in their hormones and gene expression that it could substantially promote their growth. They stroked the arabidopsis with soft paintbrushes, and then analyze the plants’ genetic responses. Within thirty minutes of being touched, 10 percent of the plant’s genome were altered. Clearly, the plant was reorganizing its priorities to deal with the disturbance, and reroute energy away from the hard work of getting taller. Touched multiple times, arabidopsis cut its upward growth rate for as much as 30 percent.",
